Subject: [#94-9-268] Higgs bosons 

In message <199409182256.AA19542@panix.com>Harry S. Hawk writes:
>a conscious being, Peter C. McCluskey wrote:
>> agnostic, due to Extropian influences. If the Higgs boson is shown
>> to be between 200 and 240 GeV (which will happen in the year 2000 if
>> Tipler is right and the Large Hadron Collider operates on schedule),
>> I may start calling myself a theist.

>Peter what is higgs boson and why would that make you call yourself
>a theist... and who would be the god you worship?

I'm not Peter, but I'll try to answer the question.  The Higgs boson
is a particle that has been predicted by certain theories of subatomic
physics.  Basically, some people discovered that you can't make a
consistent, renormalizable theory of subatomic particles if you have
terms in the equations for the particle masses.  Now, since a goodly
number of the particles we know *do* have masses, this poses a bit of
a problem.  The way out of this problem is to assume the existence of
another particle, the Higgs boson.  It interacts with the other
(massless) particles, and the strength of the interaction of the Higgs
with that particle creates an effect which acts just like that particle had
a certain mass, and does so in a way that means the theory is still
renormalizable, which means we don't have the embarassing problem of having
every number the theory produces go off to infinity. 

Now, there are other ways around this problem of particle masses; there could 
be more than one Higgs particle; there's also an alternate theory called 
"technicolor" which has bound states of two or more new particles acting in a
sort of Higgs-like way to provide particle masses.  That's why there's been a 
good deal of interest in building higher-energy particle accelerators that 
work in the range of energies where they expect the Higgs boson to exist, if 
it does.  

As for what the existence or non-existence of Higgs implies about God, I'm
not sure, but the current state of particle physics reminds me somewhat of
the story where the king of one of the kingdoms in what is now Spain was 
having astronomy (Ptolemaic epicycles) explained to him.  His comment was
"If I were God, I would have designed this more simply."  (And, of course,
Kepler and Newton at all showed a few centuries later that the planetary 
motions *did* work more simply.  Let us hope that future physicists have as
much luck making more simple explanations of the world...)

Disclaimer: I'm not a physicist, just a guy who reads a lot of science books
and sci.physics.  I've probably oversimplified a lot of things here, but I
hope the general idea comes across...



