Subject: [#94-9-269] Theoretical Applied Science

from the Howard Rheingold column: 
> I'll bet that one out of 20 of their most outlandish ideas will
> turn out to be an accurate forecast of a future technology. 

Here's another example of somebody missing the point of 
theoretical applied science.  Its purpose is not to make
"accurate forecasts" -- any Hayekian Extropian finds the
probability of of forecasting most details, and even many
general trends, to be quite low.  Its purpose is to explore 
the potentials of future technology development --
to answer questions such as: is personal immortality possible and
desirable?  Do aliens exist? Is there an overpopulation problem?  
Can climate change be stopped or reversed before it causes
damage to civilization?  What kinds of beings will humans
evolve into?  None of these questions can properly 
answered without a thorough grounding in the possibilities and 
limits of future technology -- theoretical applied science.

