From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 19 Sep 94 11:50:38 EDT
Subject: [#94-9-273] Theoretical Applied Science

Nick Szabo wrote 9/19/94: <Its purpose is to explore the potentials of future
technology development -- to answer questions such as: is personal immortality
possible and desirable?  Do aliens exist? Is there an overpopulation problem?  
Can climate change be stopped or reversed before it causes damage to
civilization?  What kinds of beings will humans evolve into?>

Prime components driving the rate of technological development are the ability
to foresee the outcomes of new technology coupled with individuals discount
factor of the future (utility).  The farther out specific technology is
accurately assessed and planned for, the greater the reward.  Unfortunately, the
farther out we plan, the greater the risk of things going haywire with our plans
if we start out even slightly off track (sensitivity to initial conditions).  If
we only plan ahead in the extremely short-term, technological development slows
to a crawl.  The optimum point of planning is for the intermediate future, where
the initial steps we take aren't as liable to go wrong, but where the rewards
are still far greater than very short-sighted planning.

Another way of looking at this, is saying that an intermediate strategy of
searching the space of all possible technology works best.  Such a strategy
combines many in the population who are doing local hill-climbing, searching for
local fitness optimums (free market mechanisms), and the few who are doing
time-consuming long jump searches seeking distant clusters of fitness optimums
possibly greater than the local cluster (the visionaries).  It takes a mix of
both to optimize both long-term technological fitness and the robustness of
technological development.

- "is personal immortality possible and desirable?"  It certainly is desirable,
any other position is death-worshipping.  Possible?  Well, it's something that
can't be observed experimentally or practically because there is no end to the
experiment.  I think a more practical wording would focus on longevity.

- "Do aliens exist?"  And have they taken over the White House?

- "Is there an overpopulation problem?"  There cannot be a philosophically
consistent position between immortality as a goal, and restrictions on the
volume of life.  Thus, there is no overpopulation problem.  There may a space
problem, a food problem, clean air and water problem but only temporarily.  All
problems are to be solved to allow for the preservation and enhancement of life.

- "Can climate change be stopped or reversed before it causes
damage to civilization?"  Or can the civilization be made more adaptable to
whatever the environment has to offer?

- "What kinds of beings will humans evolve into?"  The central question of
science is this search for universal laws matching forms and functions with all
possible environments.

